{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfjDFUdyqqZX5P1vDTWMn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The candidate passed the self-check: it printed `OK`, with a finite ELPD-LOO of −2094.7 on its short smoke fit. The model is **`heads_rigged_second_order_side_habit`**, and all three files are in `iter_4/candidate_3/`.\n\n**What I changed:** this refines the incumbent `second_order_motif_side_habit` with one change, taken from `heads_rigged_coin_suspicion`. The incumbent imagines a trick coin that is equally likely to be rigged either way, with every heads rate equally likely. In the new model, people suspect a coin rigged towards heads more than one rigged towards tails, and the model fits how strong that leaning is (the trick coin's heads rate gets a lopsided Beta prior). The rest of the incumbent is unchanged: the person-specific second-order chance coin, motif suspicion, length scaling and side habit.\n\n**Why:** it targets the one critique that survives the false-discovery correction. People pick the sequence with more heads 47.3% of the time, against the 48.8% the model predicts. Three live models already address this by making the imagined fair coin favour tails, which shifts every pair by an amount proportional to its difference in heads. Here the asymmetry sits in the rival \"trick coin\" explanation instead, so it mainly affects lopsided sequences, where the trick coin competes with the motif and switch-biased explanations. That should make its predictions distinct enough to pass the novelty gate, though the self-check doesn't test novelty, so I can't confirm it.\n\n**Things to watch at admission:**\n- The smoke fit flagged PSIS-LOO as unreliable: 7.2% of trials have high Pareto k, against a 1% tolerance. That fit was one chain of 100 draws, so this may not carry over to the full fit, but it could make the model's ELPD untrusted when it is compared.\n- Convergence was not checked, because a singl